+3. holds — src/wrap.mjs:19 `text.trim().split(/\s+/)`: trim drops leading/trailing whitespace, `\s+` treats any whitespace run (including `\n`, `\r\n`, `\t`) as one separator; test "wrapText collapses whitespace runs and drops leading/trailing whitespace" (tests/wrap.test.mjs:24-27).
+4. holds — src/wrap.mjs:24-33 greedy loop: word joins the current line iff `line.length + 1 + word.length <= width` (src/wrap.mjs:27), otherwise the line is pushed and the word starts a new one (src/wrap.mjs:30-31); test "wrapText packs words greedily" (tests/wrap.test.mjs:30-36) including the exact-fit (`"ab cd"`, 5) and one-over (`"ab cd"`, 4) boundaries.
+5. holds — a line only grows through the `<= width` check (src/wrap.mjs:27), so every multi-word line is within width; a word longer than `width` can never satisfy that check against an existing line and, once it is the current line, no later word can join it, so it always stands alone unbroken (src/wrap.mjs:25-31); tests "wrapText keeps every line within width" (tests/wrap.test.mjs:39-42) and "wrapText places a word longer than width unbroken on its own line" (tests/wrap.test.mjs:44-48).
